dy present in the range [l, r] if possible\n            // But since he moves first and can choose arbitrarily, he will try to minimize\n            // the distinct count in the final segment\n            \n            // For optimal play, Mister B should append the same letter repeatedly\n            // The best choice is to use the letter that appears most frequently in the target range\n            // But since we're looking for minimum possible, we need to consider optimal strategy\n            \n            // Actually, Mister B wants to minimize the distinct letters in [l,r]\n            // So he should append letters that are already in the range or will be in the range\n            \n            // Since Mister B moves first and can choose any letters, he can choose letters\n            // that help minimize the final count. The optimal strategy is to use the same letter\n            // that appears in the target segment to minimize new distinct letters.\n            \n            // For simplicity, let's assume Mister B appends 'a' repeatedly (optimal for minimizing)\n            for (int i = 0; i < b; i++) {\n                s.append('a');\n            }\n            \n            // Opponent's turn: append a letters based on the last a characters\n            if (s.length() >= r) break;\n            \n            String suffix = s.substring(s.length() - a);\n            Set<Character> used = new HashSet<>();\n            for (char c : suffix.toCharArray()) {\n                used.add(c);\n            }\n            \n            // Find lexicographically smallest string with distinct letters not in used\n            StringBuilder t = new StringBuilder();\n            char current = 'a';\n            while (t.length() < a) {\n                if (!used.contains(current)) {\n                    t.append(current);\n                }\n                current++;\n            }\n            s.append(t);\n        }\n        \n        // Count distinct letters in 